\section{Every percent states its denominator}{
A categorical level carries three shares, and all three are in the returned
tibble whatever the report style:
\itemize{
\item \code{pct_observed} -- over answered rows only. Its denominator excludes both
missing rows and filled ones.
\item \code{pct_valid} -- over rows that carry a value. Its denominator excludes
missing rows and includes filled ones.
\item \code{pct_total} -- over every row in the group. Its denominator includes
everything, missing rows included.
}

\code{pct_valid} and \code{pct_total} are the two a reader usually means by "the
percent", and they differ exactly when something is missing. \code{pct_observed}
differs from \code{pct_valid} exactly when something was imputed.
}

\section{Missingness cannot be switched off}{
\code{na_report} decides \strong{where} missingness is surfaced, never whether it is:
\itemize{
\item \code{"row"} -- each categorical variable gains a \code{(missing)} row, and each
numeric variable a missing line of its own.
\item \code{"column"} -- the missing count and percent appear beside every row.
\item \code{"footnote"} -- they are collected under each block, complete variables
named as such so silence is never ambiguous.
}

\code{n_missing} and \code{pct_missing} are columns of the returned tibble in all
three cases.
}

\section{Imputed values are flagged, not absorbed}{
Where the input carries indicators -- \verb{.missing_<field>} and
\verb{.imputed_<field>} from \code{\link[=sdt_read_participants]{sdt_read_participants()}}, \verb{<covariate>_missing}
and \verb{n_<covariate>} from \code{\link[=sdt_course_composition]{sdt_course_composition()}} -- each row is placed
in exactly one of three states: observed, imputed or missing. Imputed rows
are counted separately, never as observations, and numeric variables carry
\code{mean_observed}, \code{sd_observed} and \code{median_observed} alongside the
all-values statistics so both are visible at once.

For a variable with a fill rule, \code{n_imputed + n_missing} is the number of
rows nobody answered: some of them were filled and some were not, and the
two are reported apart rather than added up for the reader. That is why
\code{title_level} reports no missing values and 99 imputed ones on the TDSC
register -- an empty title box is filled with \code{none}, and the fill is
visible in every share it touches.

A value that is present in a row whose own missing indicator is set is not
an observation. It is counted as imputed when an imputation indicator or a
zero denominator (\verb{n_<covariate>} of \code{0}) says it was filled, and as
missing otherwise. That zero-denominator rule is what catches
\code{sdt_course_composition(impute = "median")}, which fills without flagging:
the fill is reported as imputed anyway, and named as unflagged.
}

\section{Level, not group}{
The participant register has no group column, so a composition descriptive
split by course is a between-course description with as many points as
there are courses. There is no split by group to be had.
}

